; v3d.pi4 - the Raspberry Pi 4's VideoCore VI (V3D 4.2) 3D core:
;           bridge witness, power, clock, reset, IDENTITY, the V3D
;           MMU, and the Texture Formatting Unit.
;
; WHAT THIS FILE DOES, AND WHERE IT DELIBERATELY STOPS.
;
;   STAGE 0 - IS THE BLOCK THERE?
;
;     0a  read the two AXI bridge ID registers. Two plain 32-bit reads
;         that need no clock, no power and no reset deassert - only
;         that the address derivation and Low Peripheral mode are
;         right. They separate "the mapping is wrong" from "the block
;         is not running", and those are different mornings' work.
;     0b  ask the firmware for the V3D power domain and the V3D clock,
;         then do the register-level reset-deassert and ASB bridge
;         enable, then read HUB_IDENT1/2/3, MMU_DEBUG_INFO and
;         CORE_IDENT0/1/2.
;
;   STAGE 1 - DOES THE BLOCK EXECUTE ANYTHING?
;
;     1a  the V3D MMU. V3D is a bus master with its own single-level
;         page table and its own TLB, and every DRAM address the
;         engine is handed goes through it. V3dMmuInit(), V3dMmuMap()
;         and V3dMmuFlush() build one and turn it on.
;     1b  the TEXTURE FORMATTING UNIT, in the hub at
;         $FEC00400-$FEC00434. Eleven register writes, no control
;         list, no shader, no binner, no tile allocation, no QPU
;         program. It reads an image out of DRAM, re-arranges it into
;         the GPU's tiled layout, and writes it back. It is the
;         smallest thing on this part that proves the GPU EXECUTED
;         something rather than that we can write registers.
;
;   IT STILL DOES NOT SUBMIT A CONTROL LIST. No binner memory, no tile
;   state, no shader, no CLE write. The reason is unchanged and is
;   written out at length under WHY THERE IS STILL NO CONTROL LIST IN
;   THIS FILE. Read that section before adding one.
;
;   A STATUS BIT IS NOT A RESULT, AND THIS FILE IS BUILT AROUND THAT.
;   V3dTfuWait() returning #V3D_OK means a counter inside the hub
;   moved. It does NOT mean the conversion happened, and it does not
;   mean the output bytes are right. The caller must read the output
;   back and check it; the diagnostic that drives this file compares
;   every one of the 256 output bytes against a layout computed
;   independently on the CPU, for exactly that reason. Every register
;   this file can offer as evidence is exposed as an accessor so that
;   a disagreement can be diagnosed rather than merely reported.
;
; THE ONE READ THIS FILE EXISTS FOR.
;
;   HUB_IDENT1 at $FEC0000C. Its low twelve bits must read $124 -
;   TVER=4, REV=2, NCORES=1 - and that single 32-bit value proves, all
;   at once: the legacy-to-ARM address derivation, Low Peripheral mode,
;   the power domain, the clock, the reset deassert and both ASB
;   bridges. It is this part's equivalent of the PCIe device ID read
;   that settled the VL805 in one line, and of GENET's SYS_REV_CTRL.
;
;   A wrong answer is also worth having, and the three shapes mean
;   different things:
;     $00000000  the block is present but dark - clock or reset
;     $FFFFFFFF  nothing answers at that address at all - mapping
;     anything else  the address is right and the part is not what
;                    this file thinks it is. Print it and stop.
;
; WHY THE GPU AT ALL, AND NOT BECAUSE THE FRAMEBUFFER IS SLOW.
;
;   IT IS NOT SLOW. Measured 2026-08-27: the same store loop into the
;   firmware framebuffer and into plain DRAM took 65.795 ms and
;   65.774 ms - a difference of twenty microseconds over eight
;   megabytes, about 190 cycles to store four bytes either way. The
;   surface is ordinary memory and writing to it costs what writing to
;   memory costs. Anybody justifying this file on "the framebuffer is
;   slow" is repeating something that has been measured and is false;
;   the wall in that loop is the A64 code generation, and the fix for
;   that is the code generator.
;
;   The honest justification is what the part can do that a CPU loop
;   cannot do at all: eight 16-lane SIMD cores at 500 MHz
;   (py-videocore6/README.md:23) running the same program on 128 pixels
;   per instruction, with a texture unit, a tile buffer with free
;   blending and depth test, and a rasteriser. A CPU loop does not get
;   faster at those; it does not do them.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THIS FILE INCLUDES NOTHING, AND DEPENDS ON mailbox.pi4
; ======================================================================
;
;  A library never includes a library. This one reads CNTPCT_EL0 and
;  CNTFRQ_EL0 directly for its timeouts, exactly as dma.pi4:729-758
;  does and for the same reason - they need no setup and no trap at the
;  exception levels a loader hands a program.
;
;  It DOES call mailbox.pi4, on the pattern clocks.pi4 established
;  (clocks.pi4's header, "DEPENDS ON mailbox.pi4"): every clock and
;  every power domain on this part belongs to the VideoCore and there
;  is no PLL register here to poke. So the PROGRAM includes both, in
;  this order:
;
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/v3d.pi4"
;
;  Nothing here is fatal if the mailbox is absent: V3dInit() records
;  that it could not ask and carries on to the register sequence, and
;  IDENT is the arbiter either way. See THE FIRMWARE IS NOT THE
;  WITNESS below.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHERE EVERY NUMBER IN THIS FILE COMES FROM
; ======================================================================
;
;  The BCM2711 ARM Peripherals manual contains exactly ONE fact about
;  V3D: section 6.2's interrupt table lists VideoCore IRQ 10 as V3D
;  (bcm2711-peripherals.txt:7963). Zero registers, zero offsets, zero
;  bitfields. Everything below is transcribed from four sources that
;  are on this disk, and every constant carries its file and line:
;
;    Datasheets/pi4/v3d/linux/v3d_regs.h
;        513 lines, one #define per register, one MASK/SHIFT pair per
;        field, version-gated where 4.2 and 7.1 differ. It is the
;        header the kernel driver includes - a datasheet chapter that
;        happens to be written in C preprocessor.
;    Datasheets/pi4/v3d/linux/bcm2835-power.c
;        the power, reset and ASB-bridge sequence, and the bridge-ID
;        probe check.
;    Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi
;        the block bases, in legacy 0x7Enn_nnnn form.
;    Datasheets/pi4/v3d/py-videocore6/v3d.py
;        a third party's working code on real Pi 4s, which maps
;        /dev/mem at the literal ARM addresses. An independent witness
;        to the derivation rather than another reading of the same
;        tree.
;
;  THE LEGACY-TO-ARM RULE, which turns 0x7Enn_nnnn into $FEnn_nnnn, is
;  the one piece the vendor manual does supply:
;  bcm2711-peripherals.txt:330 - "a peripheral described in this
;  document as being at legacy address 0x7Enn_nnnn is available in the
;  35-bit address space at 0x4_7Enn_nnnn, and visible to the ARM at
;  0x0_FEnn_nnnn if Low Peripheral mode is enabled", with the window
;  given at :322 as $FC000000 to $FF7FFFFF.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE ADDRESSES, AND THE ONE THAT COSTS AN EVENING IF IT IS WRONG
; ======================================================================
;
;  bcm2711.dtsi:605-609 - gpu@7ec00000, reg = hub 0x7ec00000 size
;  0x4000, core0 0x7ec04000 size 0x4000. Through the rule above:
;
;      hub    $FEC00000
;      core0  $FEC04000
;
;  py-videocore6/v3d.py:184 mmaps offset=0xFEC00000 length=0x4000 and
;  :193 mmaps 0xFEC04000 + 0x4000*core. Same numbers, arrived at by
;  somebody else.
;
;  bcm2711.dtsi:109-116 - the pm node carries THREE register windows,
;  named "pm", "asb" and "rpivid_asb":
;
;      PM          $FE100000  size $114
;      ASB legacy  $FE00A000  size $24
;      ASB rpivid  $FEC11000  size $20
;
;  > THE TRAP. On a BCM2711 the V3D bridge control registers are NOT in
;  > the legacy ASB at $FE00A000. bcm2835_asb_control()
;  > (bcm2835-power.c:152-166) switches the base to rpivid_asb for
;  > ASB_V3D_S_CTRL and ASB_V3D_M_CTRL specifically, and on this part
;  > rpivid_asb is present. So the two V3D bridge registers live at
;  > $FEC11008 and $FEC1100C. Using $FE00A008 / $FE00A00C instead
;  > touches a real bridge belonging to something else.
;
;  The two BRIDGE ID registers are the exception: BOTH blocks have one
;  at offset $20, and bcm2835_power_probe (bcm2835-power.c:645-658)
;  checks BOTH before it will proceed. This file does the same.
;
; ======================================================================
;  POWER: WHAT THE PI 4 ACTUALLY NEEDS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
; ======================================================================
;
;  The long BCM2835 power-up dance - POWUP, the inrush-current ramp,
;  POWOK, memory repair, ISPOW, ISFUNC - IS SKIPPED ON THIS PART.
;  Both bcm2835_power_power_on and _power_off open with
;
;      /* We don't run this on BCM2711 */
;      if (power->rpivid_asb)
;              return 0;
;
;  (bcm2835-power.c:201-202 and :226-227). The rail is already up when
;  the firmware hands over. What runs instead is bcm2835_asb_power_on
;  (:287-340), reached for BCM2835_POWER_DOMAIN_GRAFX_V3D at :383-386
;  with pm_reg = PM_GRAFX, asb_m_reg = ASB_V3D_M_CTRL,
;  asb_s_reg = ASB_V3D_S_CTRL, reset_flags = PM_V3DRSTN:
;
;    1. enable the V3D clock; wait 1 us ("Wait 32 clocks for reset to
;       propagate, 1 us will be enough", :305); disable it again
;    2. PM_GRAFX = PM_PASSWORD | (PM_GRAFX | PM_V3DRSTN)   - deassert
;    3. enable the V3D clock again
;    4. ASB_V3D_M_CTRL = PM_PASSWORD | (read & ~REQ_STOP), then spin
;       until ACK reads 0, 1 us timeout
;    5. the same for ASB_V3D_S_CTRL
;
;  EVERY WRITE TO A PM OR ASB REGISTER MUST CARRY THE PASSWORD.
;  bcm2835-power.c:100 gives PM_PASSWORD = 0x5a000000 and :110's
;  PM_WRITE macro ORs it into every write. A write without it is
;  DISCARDED BY THE HARDWARE AND REPORTS NOTHING - the register simply
;  does not change, the code carries on, and the failure surfaces
;  hundreds of instructions later as an IDENT of zero. That is why
;  V3dBringUp() reads PM_GRAFX back and refuses if the bit did not
;  stick, rather than trusting the write.
;
;  THE CLOCK STEPS ARE DONE THROUGH THE MAILBOX. The kernel's
;  clk_prepare_enable(pd->clk) is the CPRMAN V3D clock, whose registers
;  are not in the vendor manual either. The firmware owns every clock
;  on this part and mailbox.pi4 already speaks the two tags that matter
;  - $00030002 GET_CLOCK_RATE and $00038002 SET_CLOCK_RATE
;  (mailbox.pi4:524-525) - and mailbox.pi4's own header at line 212
;  already records clock id 5 = V3D. rpi-firmware.h:140-144 is the
;  second source for that number: the enum starts at EMMC = 1 and V3D
;  is the fifth entry.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE FIRMWARE IS NOT THE WITNESS. IDENT IS.
; ======================================================================
;
;  This is recorded in the tree already and it cost a wrong diagnosis
;  once. sdio.pi4:1690-1790 works it through with four witnesses:
;
;    * The firmware reported UART0 - power device id 1 - as POWERED OFF
;      in the very message the PL011 was transmitting.
;    * GET_CLOCK_STATE said the EMMC clock was off while
;      GET_CLOCK_MEASURED counted 250000496 Hz on it, twice, with
;      different jitter each time. A gated clock cannot be measured.
;    * SET_CLOCK_STATE was accepted - MBX_OK, the reply word said "on" -
;      and the state bit did not move.
;    * And the controller's own Internal Clock Stable bit came up,
;      which cannot happen in an unclocked block.
;
;  The reading, confirmed by all four: on a BCM2711 the state bit means
;  "SOME CLIENT HAS CLAIMED THIS", not "the hardware is running".
;
;  So this file NEVER treats a mailbox reply as proof and never treats
;  one as a failure. Every firmware answer is RECORDED and printed, and
;  V3dInit() carries on to the register sequence whatever they said.
;  The arbiter is HUB_IDENT1, which is the hardware answering for
;  itself. A library that had refused to continue because a domain
;  read back 0 would have stopped on the strength of a table that this
;  part demonstrably does not maintain.
;
;  BELT AND BRACES IS THE POLICY, not a fallback. V3dInit() asks the
;  firmware AND does the register sequence, and snapshots IDENT after
;  each step, so the log says which one mattered. Nothing on this disk
;  answers whether the firmware leaves V3D clocked and out of reset
;  after a bare-metal boot with no display driver loaded - the
;  feasibility study names that as stage 0's biggest unknown - and the
;  four snapshots are how one bench run answers it instead of guessing.
;
; ======================================================================
;  CACHE COHERENCY - THE PART WITH NO CITATION ANYWHERE, WORKED OUT
; ======================================================================
;
;  V3D is a bus master. It reads control lists, shaders, uniforms and
;  textures out of DRAM and writes pixels back into DRAM, while the
;  A72's caches are live. Nothing in v3d_regs.h, bcm2835-power.c,
;  v3d_packet.xml or the vendor manual says whether it is coherent with
;  the A72's L1 and L2. The kernel never has to know: it gets the
;  answer for free from dma_alloc_wc and the DMA API. Bare metal does
;  not. So here is the conclusion this file works from, with the
;  evidence, labelled as inference where it is inference:
;
;  1. V3D IS NOT IO-COHERENT WITH THE A72 ON THIS PART. Three pieces
;     of evidence, none conclusive alone and consistent together:
;       * V3D has its own L2T cache with an explicit flush register
;         and a flush ADDRESS RANGE - CTL_L2TCACTL $FEC04030,
;         CTL_L2TFLSTA $FEC04034, CTL_L2TFLEND $FEC04038
;         (v3d_regs.h:246-252 region) - and v3d_gem.c invalidates it
;         before every job. A block that snooped the CPU would not
;         need a driver-driven flush before each submission.
;       * The kernel allocates V3D's MMU scratch page with
;         dma_alloc_wc - WRITE COMBINE, i.e. uncached
;         (v3d_drv.c:325-326). Coherent hardware does not need that.
;       * V3D sits in the `scb` node, a sibling of PCIe, with no
;         dma-ranges and no coherency property (bcm2711.dtsi:545-551).
;     Treat it as NOT coherent. Being wrong that way costs cache
;     maintenance that turns out to be unnecessary; being wrong the
;     other way costs a corruption that appears seconds later, in
;     bands, and gets blamed on the renderer.
;
;  2. WITH THE MMU OFF THERE IS NO PROBLEM AT ALL, and that is the
;     correct posture for this stage. ARMv8-A treats every data access
;     as Device-nGnRnE when SCTLR.M is clear, so nothing the CPU writes
;     is ever in a cache and nothing V3D writes is ever stale. It is
;     slow and it is unambiguous, which is the right trade while the
;     question being answered is "does the block answer at all".
;
;  3. WITH THE MMU ON AND DRAM CACHEABLE the caller owns the edges,
;     exactly as dma.pi4:810-845 states for the DMA engine, and the
;     operation is the same one: `dc civac`, clean AND invalidate.
;       * before pointing V3D at anything the CPU wrote - clean, or
;         the GPU reads stale DRAM behind a dirty line;
;       * before reading back anything V3D wrote - invalidate, or the
;         CPU reads a stale line over the GPU's fresh bytes.
;     V3dCacheRange() is public for this and is the whole mechanism.
;     `dc cvac` (clean only) is not enough here because V3D buffers
;     travel in both directions. `dc ivac` is wrong twice over: it is
;     EL1-or-above, and it DISCARDS a dirty line, which would lose a
;     control list the CPU had just written.
;       THE TRAP NOBODY EXPECTS, and it is dma.pi4's, recorded again
;       here because it is a property of bus masters and not of that
;       engine: if the CPU drew into a buffer earlier with caches on
;       and those lines are still dirty, they can be evicted AFTER the
;       GPU's writes land and overwrite them. The corruption appears
;       later than the operation that caused it.
;
;  4. THE REGISTERS THEMSELVES are a different question and it is
;     already answered. mmu.pi4 maps $FC000000 upward as Device
;     (attribute #MMU_ATTR_DEVICE), so MMIO here is never cached and
;     never reordered against itself. What Device memory does NOT give
;     is ordering against DRAM, which is why V3dBarrier() exists and
;     why it is called between filling a buffer and kicking the block.
;
;  5. WHICH REGIME THE TFU CODE BELOW RUNS IN, STATED PLAINLY. The
;     diagnostic that drives it, pi4V3dTfu.pi4, runs with the ARM MMU
;     OFF - it never calls MmuEnable() and mmu.pi4 is not in its
;     include list - so case 2 applies and there is nothing to
;     maintain. Every byte the CPU writes into the input buffer is in
;     DRAM the instant the store retires, and every byte the TFU
;     writes is visible to the next load. That is the deliberate
;     choice: the question being answered is "did the GPU move these
;     bytes", and running it in the one regime where a cache cannot be
;     the explanation is what makes a mismatch mean what it says.
;
;     WHAT THE LIBRARY THEREFORE HAS TO DO ANYWAY, and does:
;     V3dTfuSubmit() takes the byte EXTENT of the input and of the
;     output, not just their addresses, and calls V3dCacheRange() over
;     both before the job and over the output after it. With the MMU
;     off those calls are `dc civac` on lines that are never allocated
;     and cost a handful of cycles each. With DRAM cacheable they are
;     the whole difference between a correct result and a corruption
;     that shows up later. Taking the extents as parameters is what
;     makes the maintenance STRUCTURAL rather than remembered - a
;     caller cannot supply a buffer without also saying how big it is,
;     and dma.pi4's history is that "the caller will remember" is the
;     assumption that fails silently.
;
;  6. THE V3D SIDE HAS CACHES OF ITS OWN AND THEY ARE A SEPARATE
;     QUESTION FROM THE A72'S. Core 0 has an L2T (texture L2) with a
;     range flush at CTL_L2TCACTL/L2TFLSTA/L2TFLEND, plus the slice
;     caches at CTL_SLCACTL, and the hub has the MMU's own TLB and the
;     MMUC. THE KERNEL DOES NOT INVALIDATE ANY OF THE CORE CACHES FOR
;     A TFU JOB: v3d_bin_job_run (v3d_sched.c:199), v3d_render_job_run
;     (:251) and the CSD path all call v3d_invalidate_caches(), and
;     v3d_tfu_job_run (:279-315) does not. The reading is that the TFU
;     lives in the HUB and does not go through a per-core cache at
;     all. V3dInvalidateCaches() is public anyway and the diagnostic
;     calls it once before the first job, because at that moment the
;     GPU has written nothing, so an invalidate cannot lose data - it
;     is free insurance against that reading being wrong, and being
;     wrong there would look exactly like "the GPU did not run".
;     THE MMU's TLB IS NOT OPTIONAL, though: page table entries the
;     CPU has just written are not visible to the engine until
;     V3dMmuFlush() has run, and that is not insurance, that is the
;     documented sequence (v3d_mmu.c:31-64).
;
;  7. NONE OF THE ABOVE IS A BENCH MEASUREMENT. It is a reading, and
;     it is the most likely cause of a stage-1 failure that looks like
;     nothing happening. The cheap experiment, once the TFU passes
;     MMU-off: run the same diagnostic with the ARM MMU on and DRAM
;     cacheable. If the first passes and the second fails, the fault
;     is here and not in the job. AND A GATE CANNOT ANSWER IT - the
;     model's memory is a flat dictionary of bytes with no caches in
;     it at all, so a64_v3d_check.py would pass with every
;     V3dCacheRange() call in this file deleted. A sibling worker
;     proved exactly that about the 91-case DMA gate on 2026-08-27.
;     Do not read a green gate as evidence about coherency.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHY THERE IS STILL NO CONTROL LIST IN THIS FILE
;
;  (Written as "WHY THERE IS NO JOB SUBMISSION IN THIS FILE" when the
;  file covered stage 0 only. The TFU below submits jobs; the argument
;  was never about jobs, it was about CONTROL LISTS, and it is
;  unchanged. Renamed rather than deleted because the reasoning is
;  what stops the next person writing a guess.)
; ======================================================================
;
;  Because the minimum viable control list is not written down
;  anywhere, and a library in this tree does not ship a guess.
;
;  The register side of submission IS fully specified and could be
;  written today: CLE_CT0QBA $FEC04160, CT0QEA $FEC04168, CT1QBA
;  $FEC04164, CT1QEA $FEC0416C, with completion polled on CLE_BFC
;  $FEC04134 and CLE_RFC $FEC04138 incrementing (v3d_regs.h:283-320,
;  the sequence in mesa/v3dx_simulator.c:504-548 cross-checked against
;  linux/v3d_sched.c:196-273). Ten writes and two counter polls.
;
;  What is NOT specified is what those two lists must CONTAIN.
;  mesa/v3d_packet.xml declares 99 packets bit-exactly and tells you
;  what each one means; it does not tell you which subset is mandatory.
;  That answer exists only inside mesa/v3dx_rcl.c and v3dx_draw.c,
;  which compute packet contents through the Gallium state tracker for
;  a full GL draw. They are evidence, not specification. Even the
;  clear-only path - v3dx_rcl.c:499-616 emit_render_layer, which is the
;  smallest render list Mesa ever emits - carries a two-iteration
;  dummy-store loop whose only justification in the file is a comment
;  about a hardware race called GFXH-1742 (:553-566), and a
;  BRANCH_TO_IMPLICIT_TILE_LIST that requires the BINNER to have
;  already run and produced a per-tile list, empty or not. So "clear a
;  buffer" is not one list, it is a bin list, a tile state buffer, a
;  tile allocation pool with a live out-of-memory protocol, and a
;  render list, and the sizes of two of those are computed rather than
;  stated.
;
;  AND A GATE CANNOT COVER IT. The model would have to be a Python
;  implementation of the CLE written from the same v3d_packet.xml the
;  library encoded from, so it would agree with the library by
;  construction and disagree with silicon for free. That is precisely
;  the failure this project has hit four times in one week - a gate
;  that passed because its model was more forgiving than the part.
;  Writing one here would manufacture confidence rather than test
;  anything.
;
;  So the honest order is: get IDENT off the board first. Then the
;  cheapest thing that proves the GPU EXECUTED something is not the
;  graphics pipeline at all - it is either the TFU in the hub
;  ($FEC00400-$FEC00434, nine to thirteen register writes, no control
;  list, no shader, no binner, completion on HUB_INT_STS.TFUC) or the
;  CSD compute dispatcher on core 0 ($FEC04904-$FEC0491C, seven
;  register writes, the last of which launches it, completion on
;  CSD_STATUS.NUM_COMPLETED). Both are specified end to end. The
;  bin/render clear is worth doing and it is not the smallest thing,
;  and calling it the smallest thing is how the estimate goes wrong.
;
;  THE TFU IS THE ONE THAT WAS TAKEN, and the CSD is not here. The
;  difference between them is one whole tool: the CSD's CFG registers
;  point at a QPU PROGRAM, so a CSD job costs a QPU assembler before
;  it costs anything else. mesa/qpu_pack.c gives every field of the
;  64-bit instruction as a MASK/SHIFT pair and py-videocore6 is a
;  working independent implementation of the same encoding, so it is
;  buildable and it is not small. The TFU needs no program at all,
;  which is exactly why it goes first.
;
; ======================================================================
;  SAFETY - WHAT THIS FILE REFUSES TO DO
; ======================================================================
;
;  * It will not write a single PM or ASB register until BOTH bridge
;    ID registers have answered $62726467. Those two reads need
;    nothing but a correct mapping, so a failure there means the
;    address derivation is wrong - and the PM block at $FE100000 also
;    contains PM_RSTC ($1C) and PM_WDOG ($24), which are how this SoC
;    is reset. Writing password-bearing words at guessed offsets into
;    that block is not a debugging step, it is a reboot.
;  * It never clears PM_V3DRSTN, never asserts REQ_STOP, and has no
;    power-off path. Turning a rail off with no consumer of it is a
;    way to make a board stop working, not a feature - the same ruling
;    mailbox.pi4:245-247 makes about the power-domain tags.
;  * Every spin has two deadlines, a tick deadline and a spin count,
;    for mailbox.pi4:839-843's reason: CNTFRQ_EL0 may read zero on a
;    board entered by something other than the usual firmware, and an
;    unbounded spin on a device that never answers is a hang with no
;    message.
;  * NOTHING IN THE STAGE 1 PATH RUNS UNTIL V3dInit() HAS SET
;    v3d_ready. The TFU is a DMA master with a page table; pointing an
;    unpowered or misidentified block at DRAM is not a failed
;    experiment. V3dMmuInit() and V3dTfuSubmit() both refuse with
;    #V3D_ERR_NOINIT rather than proceed, and V3dMmuInit() also
;    refuses if HUB_IDENT2.WITH_MMU is clear and V3dTfuSubmit() if
;    HUB_IDENT1.WITH_TFU is clear - the part telling us the unit is
;    not fitted is a better witness than any of our arithmetic.
;  * THE PAGE TABLE IS SPARSE ON PURPOSE AND VA PAGE 0 IS NEVER
;    MAPPED. V3dMmuInit() zeroes the whole table, so every page except
;    the ones V3dMmuMap() has been asked for is INVALID, and MMU_CTL
;    is programmed with PT_INVALID_ABORT set. An address this file did
;    not intend therefore faults and names itself in MMU_VIO_ADDR
;    instead of scribbling on DRAM that belongs to something else.
;    Leaving page 0 out is the kernel's rule, not an invention -
;    v3d_gem.c:276 starts its allocator at page 1 - and it means a
;    register that was never written reads as address 0 and faults
;    rather than pointing at a real buffer.
;  * IT NEVER WRITES A V3D CORE REGISTER EXCEPT THE FOUR CACHE ONES.
;    CTL_L2TFLSTA, CTL_L2TFLEND, CTL_L2TCACTL and CTL_SLCACTL, in
;    V3dInvalidateCaches(), transcribed from v3d_gem.c:20-35 and
;    :225-240. Nothing here touches the CLE, the GMP, the PTB or the
;    QPU, and the gate refuses a write to any of them by name.
;  * IT NEVER UNMASKS AN INTERRUPT BY ITSELF. HUB_INT_MSK is left as
;    found; completion is polled on the TFU_CS conversion counter,
;    which needs no interrupt at all. V3dHubIntUnmask() exists and is
;    public, because HUB_INT_STS.TFUF is worth seeing, but a library
;    that quietly asserted VideoCore IRQ 10 to a GIC it does not own
;    would be doing something the caller never asked for. The caller
;    unmasks, the caller re-masks; V3dTfuWait() works either way.
; ======================================================================


; ======================================================================
;  BLOCK BASES
; ======================================================================

; bcm2711.dtsi:607, py-videocore6/v3d.py:184
#V3D_HUB_BASE   = $FEC00000
; bcm2711.dtsi:608, py-videocore6/v3d.py:193
#V3D_CORE0_BASE = $FEC04000
; bcm2711.dtsi:113 - reg-names "pm"
#V3D_PM_BASE    = $FE100000
; bcm2711.dtsi:114 - reg-names "asb". The LEGACY bridge.
#V3D_ASB_BASE   = $FE00A000
; bcm2711.dtsi:115 - reg-names "rpivid_asb". THE ONE V3D USES.
#V3D_RPIVID_ASB_BASE = $FEC11000


; ======================================================================
;  PM AND ASB REGISTERS
; ======================================================================

#V3D_PM_GRAFX    = $10C        ; bcm2835-power.c:80
#V3D_PM_V3DRSTN  = 64          ; BIT(6), bcm2835-power.c:86
#V3D_PM_ENAB     = 4096        ; BIT(12), bcm2835-power.c:82
#V3D_PM_ISFUNC   = 32          ; BIT(5), bcm2835-power.c:87
#V3D_PM_PASSWORD = $5A000000   ; bcm2835-power.c:100

#V3D_ASB_V3D_S_CTRL  = $08     ; bcm2835-power.c:115
#V3D_ASB_V3D_M_CTRL  = $0C     ; bcm2835-power.c:116
#V3D_ASB_AXI_BRDG_ID = $20     ; bcm2835-power.c:127

#V3D_ASB_REQ_STOP = 1          ; BIT(0), bcm2835-power.c:122
#V3D_ASB_ACK      = 2          ; BIT(1), bcm2835-power.c:123

; ASCII "brdg". bcm2835-power.c:129 spells the constant BCM2835_BRDG_ID
; and comments it /* "BRDG" */; the value 0x62726467 is lower case,
; which is worth knowing before anyone "fixes" the case in a comment.
#V3D_BRDG_ID = $62726467


; ======================================================================
;  HUB REGISTERS - offsets from #V3D_HUB_BASE
; ======================================================================

#V3D_HUB_AXICFG  = $00000      ; v3d_regs.h:23
#V3D_HUB_UIFCFG  = $00004      ; v3d_regs.h:26
#V3D_HUB_IDENT0  = $00008      ; v3d_regs.h:27
#V3D_HUB_IDENT1  = $0000C      ; v3d_regs.h:29
#V3D_HUB_IDENT2  = $00010      ; v3d_regs.h:43
#V3D_HUB_IDENT3  = $00014      ; v3d_regs.h:48
#V3D_HUB_INT_STS = $00050      ; v3d_regs.h:54
#V3D_HUB_INT_CLR = $00058      ; v3d_regs.h:56

; HUB_IDENT1 fields, v3d_regs.h:30-41. Shifts as numbers, widths as
; masks already shifted down - the file states MASK and SHIFT pairs and
; these are the shift plus the field width in bits.
#V3D_IDENT1_TVER_SHIFT   = 0   ; v3d_regs.h:41, mask V3D_MASK(3,0)
#V3D_IDENT1_REV_SHIFT    = 4   ; v3d_regs.h:39, mask V3D_MASK(7,4)
#V3D_IDENT1_NCORES_SHIFT = 8   ; v3d_regs.h:37, mask V3D_MASK(11,8)
#V3D_IDENT1_NHOSTS_SHIFT = 12  ; v3d_regs.h:35, mask V3D_MASK(15,12)
#V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE       = $F
#V3D_IDENT1_WITH_L3C = $00010000 ; BIT(16), v3d_regs.h:33
#V3D_IDENT1_WITH_TFU = $00020000 ; BIT(17), v3d_regs.h:32
#V3D_IDENT1_WITH_TSY = $00040000 ; BIT(18), v3d_regs.h:31
#V3D_IDENT1_WITH_MSO = $00080000 ; BIT(19), v3d_regs.h:30

; HUB_IDENT2 fields, v3d_regs.h:44-46
#V3D_IDENT2_WITH_MMU   = $00000100 ; BIT(8), v3d_regs.h:44
#V3D_IDENT2_L3C_NKB_MASK = $FF     ; V3D_MASK(7,0), v3d_regs.h:45

; HUB_IDENT3 fields, v3d_regs.h:49-52
#V3D_IDENT3_IPIDX_SHIFT = 0    ; v3d_regs.h:52, mask V3D_MASK(7,0)
#V3D_IDENT3_IPREV_SHIFT = 8    ; v3d_regs.h:50, mask V3D_MASK(15,8)
#V3D_IDENT3_BYTE        = $FF

; The V3D MMU's own debug register, in the hub. Read here purely as a
; second witness: v3d_drv.c:262-268 reads it before IDENT and derives
; the DMA mask and VA width from it, so a sane value is independent
; confirmation that the hub is answering rather than echoing.
#V3D_MMU_DEBUG_INFO   = $01238 ; v3d_regs.h:201
#V3D_MMU_VERSION_SHIFT = 0     ; v3d_regs.h:207, mask V3D_MASK(3,0)
#V3D_MMU_VA_WIDTH_SHIFT = 4    ; v3d_regs.h:205, mask V3D_MASK(7,4)
#V3D_MMU_PA_WIDTH_SHIFT = 8    ; v3d_regs.h:203, mask V3D_MASK(11,8)

; HUB_INT_STS / _CLR bits. Only the two TFU ones are used here; the
; MMU's three are listed because a fault raises them and a reader
; comparing a printed HUB_INT_STS against this table should not have
; to go and find v3d_regs.h to decode it.
#V3D_HUB_INT_MMU_WRV = 32      ; BIT(5), v3d_regs.h:61
#V3D_HUB_INT_MMU_PTI = 16      ; BIT(4), v3d_regs.h:62
#V3D_HUB_INT_MMU_CAP = 8       ; BIT(3), v3d_regs.h:63
#V3D_HUB_INT_TFUC    = 2       ; BIT(1), v3d_regs.h:65
#V3D_HUB_INT_TFUF    = 1       ; BIT(0), v3d_regs.h:66
#V3D_HUB_INT_MSK_STS = $0005C  ; v3d_regs.h:57
#V3D_HUB_INT_MSK_SET = $00060  ; v3d_regs.h:58 - SET a bit to MASK it
#V3D_HUB_INT_MSK_CLR = $00064  ; v3d_regs.h:59 - CLEAR a bit to UNMASK
; The polarity above reads backwards and is worth stating twice.
; v3d_irq_enable (v3d_irq.c:264-276) writes ~IRQS to MSK_SET and IRQS
; to MSK_CLR, in that order: MSK_SET masks, MSK_CLR unmasks. A file
; that "enabled" an interrupt by writing MSK_SET would silently turn
; it off.


; ======================================================================
;  THE V3D MMU - hub offsets, and the page table entry format
;
;  This is the piece with no gaps anywhere. linux/v3d_mmu.c is 120
;  lines and gives the PTE format, the base register's scaling, the
;  control word and the flush sequence in full, and v3d_regs.h gives
;  every offset and bit. Nothing below is inferred.
;
;  WHY IT HAS TO BE SET UP AT ALL, since nothing in stage 0 needed it:
;  the TFU is a bus master and its addresses are VIRTUAL.
;  v3d_submit_tfu_ioctl says so in as many words - "Userspace provides
;  the register setup for the TFU, which we don't need to validate
;  since the TFU is behind the MMU" (v3d_submit.c:1054-1055). A
;  physical address written into TFU_IIA is not a physical address to
;  the hardware; it is a virtual one that will miss.
;
;  A SINGLE LEVEL, 4 KiB PAGES, ONE 32-BIT ENTRY PER PAGE, INDEXED BY
;  VA >> 12. v3d_mmu.c's file comment: "It has a single level of page
;  tables for the V3D's 4GB address space to map to AXI bus addresses,
;  thus it could need up to 4MB of physically contiguous memory to
;  store the PTEs." The kernel allocates the full 4 MB (v3d_gem.c:246)
;  because it maps arbitrary buffers anywhere. A caller here that only
;  ever hands the engine a few known pages needs only enough table to
;  cover the highest one, which is why V3dMmuInit() takes the size.
; ======================================================================

#V3D_MMUC_CONTROL          = $01000 ; v3d_regs.h:146
#V3D_MMUC_CONTROL_FLUSHING = 4      ; BIT(2), v3d_regs.h:148
#V3D_MMUC_CONTROL_FLUSH    = 2      ; BIT(1), v3d_regs.h:149
#V3D_MMUC_CONTROL_ENABLE   = 1      ; BIT(0), v3d_regs.h:150

#V3D_MMU_CTL = $01200               ; v3d_regs.h:152
#V3D_MMU_CTL_CAP_EXCEEDED       = $08000000 ; BIT(27), v3d_regs.h:153
#V3D_MMU_CTL_CAP_EXCEEDED_ABORT = $04000000 ; BIT(26), v3d_regs.h:154
#V3D_MMU_CTL_CAP_EXCEEDED_INT   = $02000000 ; BIT(25), v3d_regs.h:155
#V3D_MMU_CTL_PT_INVALID         = $00100000 ; BIT(20), v3d_regs.h:157
#V3D_MMU_CTL_PT_INVALID_ABORT   = $00080000 ; BIT(19), v3d_regs.h:158
#V3D_MMU_CTL_PT_INVALID_INT     = $00040000 ; BIT(18), v3d_regs.h:159
#V3D_MMU_CTL_PT_INVALID_ENABLE  = $00010000 ; BIT(16), v3d_regs.h:161
#V3D_MMU_CTL_WRITE_VIOLATION       = $00001000 ; BIT(12), v3d_regs.h:162
#V3D_MMU_CTL_WRITE_VIOLATION_ABORT = $00000800 ; BIT(11), v3d_regs.h:163
#V3D_MMU_CTL_WRITE_VIOLATION_INT   = $00000400 ; BIT(10), v3d_regs.h:164
#V3D_MMU_CTL_TLB_CLEARING = $80     ; BIT(7), v3d_regs.h:166
#V3D_MMU_CTL_TLB_CLEAR    = 4       ; BIT(2), v3d_regs.h:168
#V3D_MMU_CTL_ENABLE       = 1       ; BIT(0), v3d_regs.h:170

; The three STICKY status bits, as one mask. Reading MMU_CTL after a
; job that produced nothing is the cheapest way to find out that the
; engine faulted rather than idled, and it needs no interrupt.
#V3D_MMU_CTL_FAULTS = $08101000

#V3D_MMU_PT_PA_BASE = $01204        ; v3d_regs.h:172
#V3D_MMU_HIT        = $01208        ; v3d_regs.h:173
#V3D_MMU_MISSES     = $0120C        ; v3d_regs.h:174
#V3D_MMU_VIO_ID     = $0122C        ; v3d_regs.h:192 - the faulting master
#V3D_MMU_ILLEGAL_ADDR = $01230      ; v3d_regs.h:195
#V3D_MMU_ILLEGAL_ADDR_ENABLE = $80000000 ; BIT(31), v3d_regs.h:196
#V3D_MMU_VIO_ADDR   = $01234        ; v3d_regs.h:199 - the faulting VA

; v3d_drv.h:24 - V3D_MMU_PAGE_SHIFT 12. Both forms, because the shift
; is what PT_PA_BASE and the PTE want and the size is what the
; alignment checks want.
#V3D_MMU_PAGE_SHIFT = 12
#V3D_MMU_PAGE_BYTES = 4096

; Page table entry. v3d_mmu.c:26-28 defines exactly three bits and
; v3d_mmu.c:96 builds an entry as `page_prot | (dma_addr >> 12)`, with
; page_prot = WRITEABLE | VALID (:90). SUPERPAGE is defined and is not
; used here; the comment above it (:24-26) warns that ALL the PTEs of
; a 1 MB superpage must carry the bit, which is a footgun with no
; benefit at these sizes.
#V3D_PTE_SUPERPAGE = $80000000 ; BIT(31), v3d_mmu.c:27
#V3D_PTE_WRITEABLE = $20000000 ; BIT(29), v3d_mmu.c:28
#V3D_PTE_VALID     = $10000000 ; BIT(28), v3d_mmu.c:29

; v3d_mmu.c:99 - BUG_ON(page_address + ... >= BIT(24)). The physical
; page number in a PTE is TWENTY-FOUR BITS, so the highest physical
; address this MMU can reach is (2^24) * 4096 = 64 GiB. Not a limit
; anything here comes near, and worth naming because a PTE that
; overflowed into the flag bits would come out VALID by accident.
#V3D_PTE_PFN_LIMIT = $1000000


; ======================================================================
;  THE TEXTURE FORMATTING UNIT - hub offsets from #V3D_HUB_BASE
;
;  ALL OF THESE ARE THE `ver < 71` ARM of a version-gated macro.
;  v3d_regs.h writes every TFU register as
;  `((ver >= 71) ? 0x00700 : 0x00400)`, and on a V3D 7.1 part the
;  whole block moves by $300. That is the reason V3dCheckIdent()
;  refuses a part whose TVER is not 4 instead of shrugging: with the
;  7.1 offsets, writing TFU_CS at $400 hits something else entirely.
; ======================================================================

#V3D_TFU_CS    = $00400 ; v3d_regs.h:91
#V3D_TFU_SU    = $00404 ; v3d_regs.h:101
#V3D_TFU_ICFG  = $00408 ; v3d_regs.h:112
#V3D_TFU_IIA   = $0040C ; v3d_regs.h:117 - Input Image Address
#V3D_TFU_ICA   = $00410 ; v3d_regs.h:119 - Input Chroma Address
#V3D_TFU_IIS   = $00414 ; v3d_regs.h:121 - Input Image Stride
#V3D_TFU_IUA   = $00418 ; v3d_regs.h:123 - Input Image U-Plane Address
#V3D_TFU_IOA   = $0041C ; v3d_regs.h:127 - Output Image Address
#V3D_TFU_IOS   = $00420 ; v3d_regs.h:129 - Image Output Size
#V3D_TFU_COEF0 = $00424 ; v3d_regs.h:131
#V3D_TFU_COEF1 = $00428 ; v3d_regs.h:135
#V3D_TFU_COEF2 = $0042C ; v3d_regs.h:137
#V3D_TFU_COEF3 = $00430 ; v3d_regs.h:139
#V3D_TFU_CRC   = $00434 ; v3d_regs.h:142

; TFU_CS. CVTCT is the one that matters: an eight-bit count of
; CONVERSIONS COMPLETED. mesa/v3dx_simulator.c:182 and :200 use
; nothing else to decide a TFU job is done - it samples CVTCT before
; the submission and spins until it CHANGES. That is why this file
; polls it rather than HUB_INT_STS.TFUC: a counter that moved is a
; fact, and an interrupt status bit is behind a mask this file does
; not touch.
#V3D_TFU_CS_TFURST      = $80000000 ; BIT(31), v3d_regs.h:94
#V3D_TFU_CS_CVTCT_SHIFT = 16        ; v3d_regs.h:96
#V3D_TFU_CS_CVTCT_BYTE  = $FF       ; V3D_MASK(23,16), v3d_regs.h:95
#V3D_TFU_CS_NFREE_SHIFT = 8         ; v3d_regs.h:98
#V3D_TFU_CS_NFREE_6BIT  = $3F       ; V3D_MASK(13,8), v3d_regs.h:97
#V3D_TFU_CS_BUSY        = 1         ; BIT(0), v3d_regs.h:99

#V3D_TFU_ICFG_IOC      = 1          ; BIT(0), v3d_regs.h:114
#V3D_TFU_COEF0_USECOEF = $80000000  ; BIT(31), v3d_regs.h:133

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE FIELD LAYOUT OF ICFG AND IOA IS NOT IN v3d_regs.h AT ALL.
;
;  The kernel driver never composes those two words - userspace hands
;  them over already built (v3d_submit.c:1054-1055) - so its header
;  defines exactly one bit of ICFG, IOC, and nothing of IOA. Every
;  other field below comes from
;  RaspberryPi4/Reference/mesa-24.3.4_v3d_tfu.h, fetched 2026-08-27
;  because it was the one thing missing.
;
;  TWO INDEPENDENT WITNESSES USE THESE, and they agree field for
;  field: mesa-24.3.4_v3dx_tfu.c:120-155 (the Gallium driver) and
;  mesa-24.3.4_v3dvx_meta_common.c:953-1031 (the Vulkan driver). They
;  are separate code paths written for separate APIs and they compose
;  the same word the same way, which is as close to a specification as
;  this register has.
;
;  THE `V3D33_` PREFIX IS NOT A MISTAKE AND DOES NOT MEAN V3D 3.3
;  ONLY. Mesa's version macros treat 3.3 and 4.2 as one family - the
;  guards around these uses are `#if V3D_VERSION == 42` in
;  v3dx_tfu.c:122 and `#if V3D_VERSION <= 42` in
;  v3dvx_meta_common.c:953. The names were never renamed when 4.2
;  arrived. Cross-check: the same file's V3D71_ block puts the output
;  format in a separate IOC register that does not exist on 4.2, which
;  is what makes the two families distinguishable at all.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; ICFG. Reference/mesa-24.3.4_v3d_tfu.h:36-49.
#V3D_TFU_ICFG_NUMMM_SHIFT  = 5   ; :36 - (last_level - base_level)
#V3D_TFU_ICFG_TTYPE_SHIFT  = 9   ; :37 - the texture data format
#V3D_TFU_ICFG_FORMAT_SHIFT = 18  ; :41 - the INPUT tiling
#V3D_TFU_ICFG_OPAD_SHIFT   = 22  ; :39 - UIF destination padding only

; The INPUT tiling codes, ICFG bits 21:18. :42-49.
#V3D_TFU_IFMT_RASTER      = 0    ; :42
#V3D_TFU_IFMT_SAND_128    = 1    ; :43
#V3D_TFU_IFMT_SAND_256    = 2    ; :44
#V3D_TFU_IFMT_LINEARTILE  = 11   ; :45
#V3D_TFU_IFMT_UBLINEAR_1  = 12   ; :46
#V3D_TFU_IFMT_UBLINEAR_2  = 13   ; :47
#V3D_TFU_IFMT_UIF_NO_XOR  = 14   ; :48
#V3D_TFU_IFMT_UIF_XOR     = 15   ; :49

; IOA. The output ADDRESS and the output TILING share one register.
; Reference/mesa-24.3.4_v3d_tfu.h:28-34.
#V3D_TFU_IOA_DIMTW        = 1    ; :28 - skip level 0, write mipmaps only
#V3D_TFU_IOA_FORMAT_SHIFT = 3    ; :29
#V3D_TFU_OFMT_LINEARTILE  = 3    ; :30
#V3D_TFU_OFMT_UBLINEAR_1  = 4    ; :31
#V3D_TFU_OFMT_UBLINEAR_2  = 5    ; :32
#V3D_TFU_OFMT_UIF_NO_XOR  = 6    ; :33
#V3D_TFU_OFMT_UIF_XOR     = 7    ; :34

; THE OUTPUT ADDRESS THEREFORE HAS TO BE 64-BYTE ALIGNED, because bits
; 5:0 of IOA are not address. There is no RASTER code in the output
; list: mesa-24.3.4_v3dx_tfu.c:59-60 refuses the job outright when the
; destination is raster ("Can't write to raster") and
; v3dvx_meta_common.c:1016 asserts it. The TFU converts INTO the GPU's
; layouts and never back out; a caller wanting raster out wants
; something other than the TFU.
#V3D_TFU_IOA_ADDR_ALIGN = 64
#V3D_TFU_IOA_FLAG_MASK  = $3F

; IOS is (height << 16) | width, in TEXELS. Two witnesses,
; v3dx_tfu.c:92 and v3dvx_meta_common.c:944, both write it as a plain
; shift-and-or with no masking, so each half is sixteen bits.
#V3D_TFU_IOS_HEIGHT_SHIFT = 16
#V3D_TFU_IOS_DIM_MAX      = $FFFF

; IIS, for a RASTER input, is the row stride IN TEXELS - `stride /
; cpp` at v3dx_tfu.c:114 and v3dvx_meta_common.c:1009. It is NOT
; bytes, and it is not the same quantity for a tiled input, where the
; same field carries `padded_height / (2 * utile_height)` instead
; (:110-112 and :1006). This file only ever sends raster input.

; Texture data formats, for ICFG's TTYPE. From the enum in
; mesa/v3d_packet.xml:1637-1678, which is the on-disk bit-exact
; packet specification and the same enum the texture unit uses.
#V3D_TEXFMT_RGBA8 = 4  ; v3d_packet.xml:1646
#V3D_TEXFMT_R32F  = 29 ; v3d_packet.xml:1674 - "generic unfiltered
                       ; 32-bit sample"

; WHY R32F AND NOT RGBA8 FOR A COPY. TTYPE tells the TFU the texel
; SIZE, which is what fixes the microtile geometry; both of the above
; are four bytes and should therefore lay out identically. Mesa still
; rewrites the format to a generic one of the same width whenever the
; job is an exact copy rather than a mipmap fill - the switch at
; mesa-24.3.4_v3dx_tfu.c:66-77, whose comment says "there is no pixel
; format conversion. Thus we can rewrite the format to use one that is
; TFU compatible based on its texel size", and cpp 4 becomes
; PIPE_FORMAT_R32_FLOAT. "Should behave identically" is an inference
; and R32F is a citation, so the diagnostic runs the same job twice,
; once with each, and prints whether the bytes differ. If they do,
; that is a fact nothing on this disk records.


; ======================================================================
;  STAGE 2 - THE COMPUTE SHADER DISPATCHER (CSD)
;
;  Seven register writes in core 0, and the SECOND thing on this part
;  that executes. It needs no control list, no binner, no tile state,
;  no tile allocation and no rasteriser - and unlike the TFU it runs a
;  QPU PROGRAM, which is why it could not be attempted until
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/v3dqpu.pi4 existed.
;
;  THE WRITE ORDER IS PART OF THE INTERFACE. CFG1 through CFG6 first,
;  then CFG0, because "CFG0 write kicks off the job" - the kernel's
;  own comment at v3d_sched.c:356-357, and the simulator does the same
;  at v3dx_simulator.c:219-231. Writing CFG0 first would start a job
;  with six of its seven words unset.
;
;  THE ADDRESSES ARE VIRTUAL, exactly as the TFU's are. The CSD is a
;  bus master behind the same V3D MMU, and CFG5 and CFG6 are BO
;  offsets in the kernel's path, which is to say V3D virtual
;  addresses. There is no separate statement of this for the CSD the
;  way v3d_submit.c:1054-1055 gives one for the TFU, so it is an
;  INFERENCE and is marked as one - but it is an inference from the
;  same MMU in front of the same core, and V3dCsdSubmit() refuses
;  without a page table for the same reason V3dTfuSubmit() does.
;
;  Register offsets from v3d_regs.h:402-449, taking the ver < 71 arm
;  of each `(ver >= 71) ? a : b`. This part is 4.2, proven on silicon.
; ======================================================================

#V3D_CSD_STATUS = $00900       ; v3d_regs.h:402
; NUM_COMPLETED IS EIGHT BITS AND IT IS NOT A COUNT OF WORKGROUPS.
; v3dx_simulator.c:233-236: "in spite of that name that register field
; is about the number of completed DISPATCHES". It is the CSD's
; equivalent of TFU_CS.CVTCT and it is polled the same way - sample
; before, spin until it CHANGES - which needs no interrupt and no
; unmasking, so the answer does not depend on whether a masked event
; still shows in CTL_INT_STS on this part. That question was left
; open in stage 1 and it is still open.
#V3D_CSD_STATUS_NUM_COMPLETED_SHIFT = 4  ; v3d_regs.h:404
#V3D_CSD_STATUS_NUM_COMPLETED_BYTE  = $FF ; V3D_MASK(11,4), v3d_regs.h:403
#V3D_CSD_STATUS_NUM_ACTIVE_SHIFT    = 2  ; v3d_regs.h:406
#V3D_CSD_STATUS_NUM_ACTIVE_2BIT     = 3  ; V3D_MASK(3,2), v3d_regs.h:405
#V3D_CSD_STATUS_HAVE_CURRENT = 2         ; BIT(1), v3d_regs.h:407
#V3D_CSD_STATUS_HAVE_QUEUED  = 1         ; BIT(0), v3d_regs.h:408

#V3D_CSD_QUEUED_CFG0 = $00904  ; v3d_regs.h:410, ver < 71 arm
#V3D_CSD_QUEUED_CFG1 = $00908  ; v3d_regs.h:416
#V3D_CSD_QUEUED_CFG2 = $0090C  ; v3d_regs.h:422
#V3D_CSD_QUEUED_CFG3 = $00910  ; v3d_regs.h:428
#V3D_CSD_QUEUED_CFG4 = $00914  ; v3d_regs.h:440
#V3D_CSD_QUEUED_CFG5 = $00918  ; v3d_regs.h:443
#V3D_CSD_QUEUED_CFG6 = $0091C  ; v3d_regs.h:446

; CFG0, CFG1 and CFG2 are (count << 16) | offset, for X, Y and Z.
; v3d_csd.h:26-27, and v3d_regs.h:411-426 gives the same two fields
; three times over.
#V3D_CSD_CFG012_WG_COUNT_SHIFT  = 16
#V3D_CSD_CFG012_WG_OFFSET_SHIFT = 0

; CFG3, the supergroup description. v3d_csd.h:28-35 and
; v3d_regs.h:429-437.
#V3D_CSD_CFG3_OVERLAP_WITH_PREV       = $04000000 ; BIT(26)
#V3D_CSD_CFG3_MAX_SG_ID_SHIFT         = 20
#V3D_CSD_CFG3_BATCHES_PER_SG_M1_SHIFT = 12
#V3D_CSD_CFG3_WGS_PER_SG_SHIFT        = 8
; FOUR BITS, AND ZERO MEANS SIXTEEN. v3d_csd.h:33 says exactly that,
; and Mesa writes `wgs_per_sg & 0xf` (v3dx_draw.c:1524) so that
; sixteen lands as zero. py-videocore6 does NOT mask (driver.py:164):
; it writes 16 << 8, whose bit 12 falls inside BATCHES_PER_SG_M1, and
; it gets away with it only because in its own configuration that bit
; is already set by the batch count. A DIFFERENT BATCH COUNT WOULD
; CORRUPT THE FIELD. Masked here, on Mesa's rule.
#V3D_CSD_CFG3_WGS_PER_SG_NIBBLE = $F
#V3D_CSD_CFG3_WG_SIZE_SHIFT     = 0
#V3D_CSD_CFG3_WG_SIZE_BYTE      = $FF

; CFG5 is the SHADER ADDRESS with three flags in its low three bits.
; v3d_csd.h:37-40. So a QPU program that is not eight-byte aligned
; does not merely run oddly - three of its address bits mean
; something else. That is why V3dQpuProgramBegin() refuses a
; misaligned buffer, and why V3dCsdCode() refuses one again here: the
; two checks are in different files and a caller can reach the second
; without the first.
#V3D_CSD_CFG5_PROPAGATE_NANS = 4  ; BIT(2)
#V3D_CSD_CFG5_SINGLE_SEG     = 2  ; BIT(1)
#V3D_CSD_CFG5_THREADING      = 1  ; BIT(0)
#V3D_CSD_CFG5_ADDR_ALIGN     = 8
#V3D_CSD_CFG5_FLAG_MASK      = 7

; CSDDONE in CTL_INT_STS. v3d_regs.h:275, ver < 71 arm. Read as
; evidence after the fact and never waited on - see NUM_COMPLETED.
#V3D_CTL_INT_CSDDONE = 128     ; BIT(7)


; ======================================================================
;  CORE 0 REGISTERS - offsets from #V3D_CORE0_BASE
; ======================================================================

#V3D_CTL_IDENT0  = $00000      ; v3d_regs.h:211
#V3D_CTL_IDENT1  = $00004      ; v3d_regs.h:215
#V3D_CTL_IDENT2  = $00008      ; v3d_regs.h:230
#V3D_CTL_INT_STS = $00050      ; v3d_regs.h:267
#V3D_CTL_INT_CLR = $00058      ; v3d_regs.h:269
#V3D_ERR_STAT    = $00F20      ; v3d_regs.h:495

; CTL_IDENT0 field, v3d_regs.h:212-213
#V3D_CTL_IDENT0_VER_SHIFT = 24 ; mask V3D_MASK(31,24)

; CTL_IDENT1 fields, v3d_regs.h:216-228
#V3D_CTL_IDENT1_REV_SHIFT      = 0  ; v3d_regs.h:228, V3D_MASK(3,0)
#V3D_CTL_IDENT1_NSLC_SHIFT     = 4  ; v3d_regs.h:226, V3D_MASK(7,4)
#V3D_CTL_IDENT1_QUPS_SHIFT     = 8  ; v3d_regs.h:224, V3D_MASK(11,8)
#V3D_CTL_IDENT1_NTMU_SHIFT     = 12 ; v3d_regs.h:222, V3D_MASK(15,12)
#V3D_CTL_IDENT1_NSEM_SHIFT     = 16 ; v3d_regs.h:220, V3D_MASK(23,16)
#V3D_CTL_IDENT1_VPM_SIZE_SHIFT = 28 ; v3d_regs.h:218, V3D_MASK(31,28)
#V3D_CTL_IDENT1_BYTE           = $FF


; ======================================================================
;  CORE 0's CACHE CONTROL - the only core registers this file writes
;
;  Transcribed from v3d_gem.c. v3d_init_core (:20-35) sets the L2T
;  flush RANGE once, to everything, so a later flush needs one write
;  and not three; v3d_invalidate_caches (:229-240) is the
;  before-a-job sequence. On a V3D 4.2 two of its four steps are
;  no-ops by their own guards - v3d_flush_l3 does nothing when
;  ver >= 41 (:135) and v3d_invalidate_l2c does nothing when ver > 32
;  (:154-155) - so what is left is the L2T flush and the slice
;  invalidate, and those two are what is implemented here. Writing the
;  other two anyway would mean writing GCA registers that do not exist
;  on this part.
; ======================================================================

#V3D_CTL_SLCACTL  = $00024      ; v3d_regs.h:243
#V3D_CTL_L2TCACTL = $00030      ; v3d_regs.h:253
#V3D_CTL_L2TFLSTA = $00034      ; v3d_regs.h:264
#V3D_CTL_L2TFLEND = $00038      ; v3d_regs.h:265

#V3D_L2TCACTL_TMUWCF    = $100  ; BIT(8), v3d_regs.h:254
#V3D_L2TCACTL_L2TFLS    = 1     ; BIT(0), v3d_regs.h:263
#V3D_L2TCACTL_FLM_FLUSH = 0     ; v3d_regs.h:256 - invalidate lines
#V3D_L2TCACTL_FLM_CLEAR = 1     ; v3d_regs.h:258 - drop, do not write back
#V3D_L2TCACTL_FLM_CLEAN = 2     ; v3d_regs.h:260 - WRITE BACK dirty lines
#V3D_L2TCACTL_FLM_SHIFT = 1     ; v3d_regs.h:262, mask V3D_MASK(2,1)

; THE THREE FLUSH MODES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE AND THE NAMES INVITE
; THE MISTAKE. v3d_regs.h says it plainly at :255-261:
;   FLUSH (0) "Invalidates cache lines"      - throws content away
;   CLEAR (1) "Removes cachelines WITHOUT writing dirty lines back"
;   CLEAN (2) "WRITES OUT dirty cachelines and marks them clean, but
;              doesn't invalidate"
; ONLY CLEAN MOVES DATA OUT OF THE GPU. V3dInvalidateCaches() uses
; FLUSH, which is right for "the GPU is about to read what the CPU
; wrote" and USELESS for "the CPU is about to read what the GPU
; wrote". See V3dCleanCaches() below and the bench log that made the
; difference concrete.

; All four slice-cache clear fields set: TVCCS 27:24, TDCCS 19:16,
; UCC 11:8, ICC 3:0, each written as $F (v3d_gem.c:221-226 through
; V3D_SET_FIELD, v3d_regs.h:244-251). Composed here as the finished
; word with the field positions named above it, because the four
; shifts ARE the whole content of the constant and a reader checking
; it against the header wants to see $0F0F0F0F.
#V3D_SLCACTL_ALL = $0F0F0F0F

; L2TFLEND takes ~0 in v3d_init_core (:35): flush EVERYTHING, always.
; Named rather than written as a bare $FFFFFFFF in the middle of a
; sequence, where it reads like a mistake.
#V3D_L2TFL_END_ALL = $FFFFFFFF


; ======================================================================
;  MAILBOX TAGS AND IDS THIS FILE USES THROUGH THE ESCAPE HATCH
;
;  mailbox.pi4 wraps GET_CLOCK_RATE / SET_CLOCK_RATE / the measured and
;  min/max variants and nothing else this file needs. The rest go
;  through MailboxBegin / MailboxSetWord / MailboxSend / MailboxWord,
;  which is that file's documented way to issue any tag without
;  editing it (mailbox.pi4:273-291).
;
;  Each tag is cited to BOTH on-disk headers that carry the enum, the
;  way sdio.pi4:1200-1207 cites its six. "h:" is
;  Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/raspberrypi-firmware-downstream.h and "rf:" is
;  Datasheets/pi4/v3d/linux/rpi-firmware.h.
; ======================================================================

#V3D_TAG_GET_POWER_STATE  = $00020001  ; h:52  rf:50
#V3D_TAG_SET_POWER_STATE  = $00028001  ; h:54  rf:52
#V3D_TAG_GET_CLOCK_STATE  = $00030001  ; h:55
#V3D_TAG_SET_CLOCK_STATE  = $00038001  ; h:81
#V3D_TAG_GET_DOMAIN_STATE = $00030030  ; h:77  rf:74
#V3D_TAG_SET_DOMAIN_STATE = $00038030  ; h:86  rf:83

; Both families take { u32 id, u32 state } in and out.
#V3D_MBX_TWO_WORDS = 8
#V3D_MBX_STATE_ON  = 1

; THE CLOCK ID. rpi-firmware.h:140-144 - the enum opens at
; RPI_FIRMWARE_EMMC_CLK_ID = 1 and V3D is the fifth entry, so 5.
; mailbox.pi4's own header at line 212 records the same list.
#V3D_CLOCK_ID = 5

; TWO DIFFERENT NUMBERS FOR THE SAME BLOCK, AND THEY ARE NOT
; INTERCHANGEABLE. This is the kind of thing that eats an afternoon.
;
;   * The NEW interface, SET_DOMAIN_STATE $00038030, wants domain
;     ELEVEN. rpi-power-bindings.h:9-11 says in as many words "These
;     power domain indices are the firmware interface's indices minus
;     one", :22 gives RPI_POWER_DOMAIN_V3D = 10, and
;     raspberrypi-power.c:120 does the arithmetic explicitly:
;     dom->domain = xlate_index + 1.
;   * The OLD interface, SET_POWER_STATE $00028001, wants device TEN.
;     raspberrypi-power.c:21 - RPI_OLD_POWER_DOMAIN_V3D 10 - a separate
;     constant defined in that file precisely because the two numbering
;     schemes disagree.
;
; Both are issued, because they are cheap and because the firmware's
; answer to each is a fact worth having in the log. Neither is treated
; as proof of anything; see THE FIRMWARE IS NOT THE WITNESS.
#V3D_DOMAIN_ID_NEW = 11
#V3D_POWER_ID_OLD  = 10


; ======================================================================
;  ERROR CODES
;
;  Numbered in the order the bring-up reaches them, so the number alone
;  says how far it got.
; ======================================================================

#V3D_OK                = 0
#V3D_ERR_BRIDGE_LEGACY = 1   ; $FE00A020 did not read "brdg"
#V3D_ERR_BRIDGE_RPIVID = 2   ; $FEC11020 did not read "brdg"
#V3D_ERR_PM_DEAD       = 3   ; PM_GRAFX read all-ones - nothing there
#V3D_ERR_RESET         = 4   ; V3DRSTN did not read back set
#V3D_ERR_ASB_MASTER    = 5   ; ASB_V3D_M_CTRL ACK never cleared
#V3D_ERR_ASB_SLAVE     = 6   ; ASB_V3D_S_CTRL ACK never cleared
#V3D_ERR_IDENT_ZERO    = 7   ; HUB_IDENT1 reads $00000000 - dark
#V3D_ERR_IDENT_ONES    = 8   ; HUB_IDENT1 reads $FFFFFFFF - absent
#V3D_ERR_IDENT_VERSION = 9   ; present, but not TVER=4 REV=2
#V3D_ERR_IDENT_CORES   = 10  ; present, right version, NCORES not 1
#V3D_ERR_NOINIT        = 11  ; asked for a result before V3dInit()

; STAGE 1. Numbered on from stage 0's, and again in the order the
; sequence reaches them, so the number alone says how far it got.
#V3D_ERR_TFU_ABSENT    = 12  ; HUB_IDENT1.WITH_TFU is clear
#V3D_ERR_MMU_ABSENT    = 13  ; HUB_IDENT2.WITH_MMU is clear
#V3D_ERR_MMU_ALIGN     = 14  ; a page table, page or buffer is not
                             ; 4 KiB aligned, or a TFU address is not
                             ; 64-byte aligned
#V3D_ERR_MMU_RANGE     = 15  ; a VA outside the table, a PFN past the
                             ; PTE's 24 bits, or VA page 0
#V3D_ERR_MMU_FLUSH     = 16  ; TLB clear or MMUC flush never finished
#V3D_ERR_MMU_OFF       = 17  ; a TFU job offered before V3dMmuInit()
#V3D_ERR_TFU_ARGS      = 18  ; a job description that cannot be encoded
#V3D_ERR_TFU_BUSY      = 19  ; TFU_CS.BUSY was set before submission
#V3D_ERR_TFU_TIMEOUT   = 20  ; TFU_CS.CVTCT never advanced
#V3D_ERR_TFU_FAIL      = 21  ; HUB_INT_STS.TFUF - the unit said no
#V3D_ERR_MMU_FAULT     = 22  ; MMU_CTL raised PT_INVALID, a write
                             ; violation or cap exceeded

; STAGE 2, the compute dispatcher. Numbered on again.
#V3D_ERR_CSD_ARGS      = 23  ; a dispatch that cannot be encoded - a
                             ; zero workgroup count, a workgroup size
                             ; outside eight bits, a misaligned shader
#V3D_ERR_CSD_BUSY      = 24  ; CSD_STATUS said a dispatch was already
                             ; current or queued before submission
#V3D_ERR_CSD_TIMEOUT   = 25  ; NUM_COMPLETED never advanced
#V3D_ERR_CACHE_TIMEOUT = 26  ; the TMU write combiner or the L2T clean
                             ; never reported itself finished

; What the low twelve bits of HUB_IDENT1 must be on this part:
; TVER=4, REV=2, NCORES=1. v3d_drv.c:297-300 computes
; ver = TVER*10 + REV and the part is documented as V3D 4.2, so
; TVER=4 and REV=2, giving a low byte of $24; NCORES=1 puts a 1 in
; bits 11:8. THIS IS A PREDICTION MADE IN ADVANCE OF THE READ and it
; is the pass criterion. The upper bits are NOT predicted - the
; capability bits and NHOSTS are what the read is for.
#V3D_IDENT1_EXPECT_LOW12 = $124
#V3D_IDENT1_LOW12_MASK   = $FFF
#V3D_EXPECT_TVER   = 4
#V3D_EXPECT_REV    = 2
#V3D_EXPECT_NCORES = 1

; The second witness, on core 0. py-videocore6/README.md:23 gives the
; array as "2 [slice] x 4 [qpu/slice]" for VideoCore VI, so NSLC must
; read 2 and QUPS must read 4. Also predicted in advance; also not a
; refusal, because a part that answered IDENT correctly and then
; disagreed about its own geometry is a finding to print rather than
; a reason to stop.
#V3D_EXPECT_NSLC = 2
#V3D_EXPECT_QUPS = 4


; ======================================================================
;  TIMEOUTS
;
;  Two deadlines on every spin, dma.pi4:906-908's shape and
;  mailbox.pi4:839-843's reason: the tick deadline is the real one and
;  the spin count is the backstop for a board where CNTFRQ_EL0 reads
;  zero because nothing set it.
;
;  The kernel's ASB timeout is ONE MICROSECOND (bcm2835-power.c:180-183
;  compares against 1000 ns). A thousand microseconds here is a
;  thousand times looser, deliberately: this code may be running with
;  the caches off at 600 MHz where a read-modify-write of a Device
;  register is not free, and a timeout that is tight enough to trip on
;  a slow bus would produce a failure that is about this file rather
;  than about the hardware. A bridge that has not acknowledged in a
;  millisecond is not slow, it is not there.
; ======================================================================

#V3D_ASB_TIMEOUT_US = 1000
#V3D_SPIN_LIMIT     = 20000000

; "Wait 32 clocks for reset to propagate, 1 us will be enough" -
; bcm2835-power.c:304-305. Ten, for the same reason as above.
#V3D_RESET_SETTLE_US = 10

; The A72's cache line. Fixed at 64 bytes on this part; mailbox.pi4
; carries the same constant as #MBX_CACHE_LINE (mailbox.pi4:882) and
; dma.pi4 as #DMA_CACHE_LINE, and this file has its own because a
; library never includes a library.
#V3D_CACHE_LINE = 64


; ======================================================================
;  STAGE 1 TIMEOUTS
;
;  ONE HUNDRED MILLISECONDS, WHICH IS THE KERNEL'S OWN NUMBER FOR THE
;  MMU. v3d_mmu_flush_all writes wait_for(cond, 100) four times
;  (v3d_mmu.c:37, :50, :58), and v3d_drv.h:488 expands wait_for(C, MS)
;  to a MS * 1000 microsecond budget. So 100 ms, transcribed, not
;  chosen.
;
;  THE TFU DEADLINE IS A CHOICE AND SAYS SO. Nothing on this disk puts
;  a number on how long a TFU conversion takes; the kernel waits on an
;  interrupt with only the scheduler's job timeout behind it. The
;  conversions this file is built for are 256 bytes, which at any
;  plausible clock is well under a microsecond, so 100 ms is five
;  orders of magnitude of slack. It is a BOUND, not a schedule: its
;  only job is to turn a unit that never answers into a named error
;  instead of a board that stops printing. Reusing the MMU's number
;  keeps one constant to reason about rather than two.
; ======================================================================

#V3D_MMU_FLUSH_TIMEOUT_US = 100000  ; v3d_mmu.c:37 via v3d_drv.h:488
#V3D_TFU_TIMEOUT_US       = 100000  ; a bound, see above


; ======================================================================
;  STATE
;
;  All of it is diagnostic. Nothing here is a cached answer that a
;  later call trusts instead of reading the hardware again - the
;  snapshots exist so that ONE bench run can say which step of the
;  bring-up was the one that mattered, which is the question nothing on
;  this disk answers.
; ======================================================================

Global v3d_err.i     = #V3D_OK
Global v3d_ready.i   = 0        ; 1 once IDENT has answered correctly

Global v3d_brdgLegacy.i = 0     ; $FE00A020 as read
Global v3d_brdgRpivid.i = 0     ; $FEC11020 as read

; HUB_IDENT1 at four points in the sequence. THIS IS THE INSTRUMENT.
Global v3d_ident1AtEntry.i    = 0  ; before anything is asked or written
Global v3d_ident1AfterClock.i = 0  ; after the mailbox clock/domain asks
Global v3d_ident1AfterReset.i = 0  ; after PM_GRAFX V3DRSTN is deasserted
Global v3d_ident1AfterAsb.i   = 0  ; after both ASB bridges are enabled

Global v3d_ident0.i  = 0
Global v3d_ident1.i  = 0
Global v3d_ident2.i  = 0
Global v3d_ident3.i  = 0
Global v3d_mmuDebug.i = 0
Global v3d_coreIdent0.i = 0
Global v3d_coreIdent1.i = 0
Global v3d_coreIdent2.i = 0

Global v3d_grafxBefore.i = 0    ; PM_GRAFX as found
Global v3d_grafxAfter.i  = 0    ; PM_GRAFX after the deassert, read back
Global v3d_asbMBefore.i  = 0
Global v3d_asbMAfter.i   = 0
Global v3d_asbSBefore.i  = 0
Global v3d_asbSAfter.i   = 0

; Everything the firmware said, recorded and believed about nothing.
Global v3d_mbxUsed.i        = 0   ; 1 if the mailbox was reachable
Global v3d_domainBefore.i   = -1  ; GET_DOMAIN_STATE, domain 11
Global v3d_domainReply.i    = -1  ; SET_DOMAIN_STATE reply word
Global v3d_domainAfter.i    = -1
Global v3d_powerBefore.i    = -1  ; GET_POWER_STATE, old device 10
Global v3d_powerReply.i     = -1
Global v3d_clockStateBefore.i = -1 ; GET_CLOCK_STATE, clock 5
Global v3d_clockStateAfter.i  = -1
Global v3d_clockRate.i      = 0   ; GET_CLOCK_RATE, clock 5
Global v3d_clockMax.i       = 0
Global v3d_clockApplied.i   = 0   ; what SET_CLOCK_RATE actually gave
Global v3d_clockMeasured.i  = 0   ; the only honest one - counted edges

Global v3d_flushaddr.i = 0        ; the one line V3dCacheLine() maintains


; ======================================================================
;  THE ARCHITECTURAL COUNTER
;
;  Read directly, dma.pi4:729-758's pattern and mailbox.pi4:925-933's
;  reason: CNTPCT_EL0 and CNTFRQ_EL0 need no setup and no trap at the
;  exception levels a loader hands a program, which makes them the
;  cheapest honest clock a library with no dependencies can have.
;  armstub8.S sets CNTFRQ_EL0 from OSC_FREQ, 54000000 on BCM2711
;  (Datasheets/pi4/armstub8.S:54 and :110-112).
;
;  THE NAMES ARE V3dTicks() AND V3dCounterHz() ON PURPOSE, exactly as
;  dma.pi4:744-747 explains for its own pair: timer.pi4 owns Ticks()
;  and TickHz() and reads the same two registers, and a program that
;  includes both files must not find two definitions of one name.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i V3dTicks()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntpct_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dCounterHz()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntfrq_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  V3dDelayUs - a bounded busy wait.
;
;  Bounded twice, for the reason under TIMEOUTS. If CNTFRQ_EL0 reads
;  zero the tick arithmetic is meaningless, so the spin count is what
;  ends the loop, and it ends it having waited SOMETHING rather than
;  nothing - which is what the reset-propagation wait actually needs.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure V3dDelayUs(us.i)
  Protected hz.i
  Protected deadline.i
  Protected spins.i

  hz = V3dCounterHz()
  spins = 0
  If hz > 0
    deadline = V3dTicks() + ((hz / 1000000) * us)
    While V3dTicks() < deadline
      spins = spins + 1
      If spins > #V3D_SPIN_LIMIT
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
    Wend
  Else
    While spins < (us * 100)
      spins = spins + 1
    Wend
  EndIf
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  BARRIERS AND CACHE MAINTENANCE
;
;  V3dBarrier() is the intrinsic a64_barrier() - dsb sy followed by
;  isb, from RaspberryPi4/Intrinsics/bcm2711_hardware.def:26-33. It is
;  wrapped rather than called directly at every site so that the
;  REASON can sit in one place: the register writes below are to Device
;  memory and are ordered against each other by the memory type, but
;  they are NOT ordered against DRAM. Every point where this file
;  hands the block something in memory, or reads back something the
;  block put there, needs the barrier and not just the Device ordering.
; ======================================================================

Procedure V3dBarrier()
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  V3dCacheLine - clean AND invalidate the one line named by the global.
;
;  The global is how the address reaches the assembly: this dialect has
;  no way to bind a procedure parameter to a register inside an ASM
;  block, so the address is parked in a Global and read back with an
;  ADRP/ADD pair. dma.pi4:799-806 does exactly this and its header
;  explains the encoding; the same note applies here.
;
;  "ldr x9", NOT "ldr w9". v3d_flushaddr is a .i and a .i is EIGHT
;  bytes on this target. `ldr w9` assembles cleanly, uses the right
;  register, and silently drops the top half of the address. Every
;  file in this tree that hand-writes this sequence says so, because
;  it is the shape of bug the widening creates.
;
;  `dc civac` and not `dc cvac` or `dc ivac` - the choice is worked
;  through under CACHE COHERENCY in the header. Buffers here travel in
;  both directions.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure V3dCacheLine()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_v3d_flushaddr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_v3d_flushaddr
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    dc civac, x9
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  V3dCacheRange - clean and invalidate every line the range touches,
;  then one barrier for the whole range.
;
;  PUBLIC, and a caller who ever points V3D at memory will need it.
;  This file maintains nothing of its own in DRAM - it only touches
;  registers - so this procedure exists entirely for the caller, which
;  is unusual enough to state.
;
;  The start rounds DOWN and the end UP, which is what U-Boot's
;  cache_v8.c does and for the same reason: a partial line still has
;  to be maintained. Rounding out can only touch a neighbouring line,
;  and clean-and-invalidate of a neighbour costs a refill and loses
;  nothing, because civac writes dirty data back before dropping it.
;
;  ROUNDED DOWN BY DIVIDE-AND-MULTIPLY, NOT BY MASKING. A mask written
;  as "addr & $FFFFFFC0" is a 32-bit literal and would clear the top
;  half of a 64-bit address along with the low six bits. The addresses
;  here are physical and therefore positive, so the divide is exact.
;  dma.pi4:823-828 records the same trap.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure V3dCacheRange(addr.i, len.i)
  Protected a.i
  Protected e.i
  If len <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  a = (addr / #V3D_CACHE_LINE) * #V3D_CACHE_LINE
  e = addr + len
  While a < e
    v3d_flushaddr = a
    V3dCacheLine()
    a = a + #V3D_CACHE_LINE
  Wend
  V3dBarrier()
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  REGISTER ACCESS
;
;  PeekN is the unsigned 32-bit read - the .n type exists precisely so
;  a register with bit 31 set does not arrive as a negative 64-bit
;  value - and PokeL is the 32-bit write. A bare Peek or Poke would be
;  EIGHT bytes on this target and would take the neighbouring register
;  with it; genet.pi4:1247-1279 and gpio.pi4:64-66 both record that
;  trap, and here it would be worse than usual: HUB_IDENT1 sits between
;  IDENT0 and IDENT2, and an eight-byte read at $FEC00008 would return
;  a plausible-looking value made of two registers.
;
;  There is no offset validation, and the reason is dma.pi4:860-864's:
;  no offset in this file comes from a caller. Every one is a named
;  constant from the tables above.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i V3dHubRead(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#V3D_HUB_BASE + off)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dCoreRead(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#V3D_CORE0_BASE + off)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dPmRead(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#V3D_PM_BASE + off)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  V3dPmWrite - a PM register write, WITH THE PASSWORD.
;
;  There is no unpassworded variant and there must never be one. A PM
;  write whose top byte is not $5A is discarded by the hardware and
;  reports nothing at all: the register does not change, no fault is
;  raised, execution continues, and the consequence surfaces hundreds
;  of instructions later as an IDENT of zero. Making the password
;  structurally impossible to omit is the only defence, because the
;  failure has no symptom at the point of the mistake.
;
;  THE MASK IS TWENTY-FOUR BITS BECAUSE THE TOP BYTE *IS* THE PASSWORD
;  FIELD. $5A000000 occupies bits 31:24, so a value carrying anything
;  up there would corrupt the password and the write would be
;  discarded - the exact silent failure this procedure exists to make
;  impossible. Masking the value first, rather than relying on PokeL's
;  truncation after the OR, makes that structural instead of lucky.
;  PM_GRAFX's own fields stop at bit 14 (INRUSH is 14:13,
;  bcm2835-power.c:94-97), so 24 bits is generous.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure V3dPmWrite(off.i, value.i)
  PokeL(#V3D_PM_BASE + off, #V3D_PM_PASSWORD | (value & $00FFFFFF))
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dAsbRead(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#V3D_RPIVID_ASB_BASE + off)
EndProcedure

; Same rule as V3dPmWrite. bcm2835_asb_control writes
; `PM_PASSWORD | val` to the ASB register (bcm2835-power.c:177), so the
; ASB block wants the same password the PM block does even though it is
; a different peripheral at a different base.
Procedure V3dAsbWrite(off.i, value.i)
  PokeL(#V3D_RPIVID_ASB_BASE + off, #V3D_PM_PASSWORD | (value & $00FFFFFF))
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  STAGE 0a - THE BRIDGE WITNESS
;
;  Two plain 32-bit reads. They need no clock, no power sequence and no
;  reset deassert - only that the address derivation and Low Peripheral
;  mode are right. They sit one step earlier in the chain than IDENT
;  and they separate "the address arithmetic is wrong" from "the block
;  is not powered", which are completely different problems.
;
;  This is not an invented check. bcm2835_power_probe does exactly it,
;  on exactly these two registers, and refuses to bind the driver if
;  either disagrees (bcm2835-power.c:645-658).
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i V3dBridgeIdLegacy()
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#V3D_ASB_BASE + #V3D_ASB_AXI_BRDG_ID)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dBridgeIdRpivid()
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#V3D_RPIVID_ASB_BASE + #V3D_ASB_AXI_BRDG_ID)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  V3dBridgeCheck() - read both, record both, return #V3D_OK or the
;  error naming which one failed.
;
;  BOTH are checked and not just the rpivid one, even though only the
;  rpivid block carries the V3D control registers. The legacy read at
;  $FE00A020 is in a completely different part of the peripheral
;  window, so the two together test the mapping much more strongly than
;  either alone: one right and one wrong says the window is partly
;  mapped, which is a different diagnosis from both wrong.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i V3dBridgeCheck()
  v3d_brdgLegacy = V3dBridgeIdLegacy()
  v3d_brdgRpivid = V3dBridgeIdRpivid()

  If v3d_brdgLegacy <> #V3D_BRDG_ID
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_BRIDGE_LEGACY
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_BRIDGE_LEGACY
  EndIf
  If v3d_brdgRpivid <> #V3D_BRDG_ID
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_BRIDGE_RPIVID
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_BRIDGE_RPIVID
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #V3D_OK
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE MAILBOX SIDE - ASKED, RECORDED, NEVER BELIEVED
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  v3d_MbxIdState - one { id, state } transaction, the shape all four
;  of the state tag families share.
;
;  sdio.pi4:1642-1658 has the identical helper and this is deliberately
;  the same shape rather than a cleverer one, so that a reader who
;  knows that file recognises this one. It is not shared code because a
;  library never includes a library.
;
;  Returns the reply's SECOND word - the state - or -1 if the
;  transaction did not complete. -1 and not 0, because 0 is a perfectly
;  good answer meaning "off" and the two must be distinguishable.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i v3d_MbxIdState(tag.i, id.i, state.i, sendState.i)
  If MailboxReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  v3d_mbxUsed = 1
  If MailboxBegin(tag, #V3D_MBX_TWO_WORDS, #V3D_MBX_TWO_WORDS) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, id)
  If sendState <> 0
    MailboxSetWord(1, state)
  EndIf
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MailboxWord(1)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  V3dQueryDomainState() / V3dQueryPowerState() / V3dQueryClockState()
;
;  PUBLIC, because the ONE thing no file on this disk settles is
;  whether the firmware maintains any of these tables on a BCM2711, and
;  a diagnostic that prints all three side by side against an IDENT
;  that either answered or did not is how that gets settled in one
;  bench run rather than argued about.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i V3dQueryDomainState()
  ProcedureReturn v3d_MbxIdState(#V3D_TAG_GET_DOMAIN_STATE, #V3D_DOMAIN_ID_NEW, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dQueryPowerState()
  ProcedureReturn v3d_MbxIdState(#V3D_TAG_GET_POWER_STATE, #V3D_POWER_ID_OLD, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dQueryClockState()
  ProcedureReturn v3d_MbxIdState(#V3D_TAG_GET_CLOCK_STATE, #V3D_CLOCK_ID, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  V3dAskFirmware() - everything this file asks the firmware for, in
;  one place, in the order the kernel's sequence implies.
;
;  RETURNS NOTHING AND FAILS AT NOTHING. Every answer lands in a global
;  and the caller carries on. That is not laziness; it is the ruling
;  under THE FIRMWARE IS NOT THE WITNESS. On this part these tables
;  have been observed reporting a transmitting UART as unpowered and a
;  measurably running clock as stopped, so a refusal built on top of
;  one of them would be a refusal built on noise.
;
;  THE CLOCK IS SET TO ITS OWN REPORTED MAXIMUM rather than to a
;  number chosen here. clocks.pi4's header explains why any wrapper
;  must return what was APPLIED rather than a boolean - the firmware
;  CLAMPS silently and reports SUCCESS - and the same applies to
;  reading it back: only MEASURED is an observation, the rest are
;  intentions. Asking for the max means config.txt's ceiling is what
;  we get, which is the only number worth asking for.
;
;  Why set the clock at all when the register sequence is what matters:
;  because bcm2835_asb_power_on's steps 1 and 3 ARE clock enables, and
;  the mailbox is the only route this project has to the CPRMAN V3D
;  clock. Setting a rate is the closest available equivalent to
;  clk_prepare_enable. Whether it is equivalent ENOUGH is one of the
;  things the four IDENT snapshots are there to answer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure V3dAskFirmware()
  Protected mx.i

  If MailboxReady() = 0
    If MailboxInit() = 0
      ; No mailbox. Recorded by v3d_mbxUsed staying 0. The register
      ; sequence below does not need it and runs anyway.
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
  EndIf
  v3d_mbxUsed = 1

  ; --- what the firmware thinks now, before anything is asked of it --
  v3d_domainBefore     = V3dQueryDomainState()
  v3d_powerBefore      = V3dQueryPowerState()
  v3d_clockStateBefore = V3dQueryClockState()
  v3d_clockRate        = MailboxClockRate(#V3D_CLOCK_ID)
  v3d_clockMax         = MailboxClockRateMax(#V3D_CLOCK_ID)

  ; --- claim the domain -----------------------------------------------
  ;
  ;  THE SECOND CLAIM USED TO BE UNCONDITIONAL AND IT KILLED THE
  ;  FIRMWARE. Measured on the board, 2026-08-28, by
  ;  pi4NeonMbxTag.pi4, which sends each bring-up tag on its own with a
  ;  full DisplayInit after it:
  ;
  ;      after the five reads
  ;          DisplayInit rc 1   SURFACE OK
  ;      SET_DOMAIN_STATE, id 11, on      reply 1
  ;          DisplayInit rc 1   SURFACE OK
  ;      SET_POWER_STATE, id 10, on       reply -2
  ;          DisplayInit rc -19  MailboxError 5   *** NO SURFACE ***
  ;
  ;  #MBX_ERR_RXTIMEOUT = 5: the request went out and NOTHING CAME
  ;  BACK. From that point the VideoCore never answers the property
  ;  mailbox again - MAIL1 fills with requests nobody consumes, every
  ;  later transaction ends in a timeout, and ONLY A BOARD RESET
  ;  clears it. It survives the payload returning to the monitor, so
  ;  the NEXT program cannot get a framebuffer either. That is a whole
  ;  session's worth of confusing symptoms from one redundant tag.
  ;
  ;  IT WAS ALWAYS REDUNDANT ON THIS FIRMWARE. SET_DOMAIN_STATE with
  ;  the new numbering answers 1 and the domain is on; the old
  ;  SET_POWER_STATE was belt and braces for a firmware that does not
  ;  know the new tag. So it is now the FALLBACK it should always have
  ;  been: sent only when the new tag did not work.
  ;
  ;  v3d_powerReply is left at -1 to mean "not attempted", which is a
  ;  different thing from the -2 that means "attempted and the
  ;  transport failed", and both are different from a real reply.
  v3d_domainReply = v3d_MbxIdState(#V3D_TAG_SET_DOMAIN_STATE, #V3D_DOMAIN_ID_NEW, #V3D_MBX_STATE_ON, 1)
  v3d_powerReply  = -1
  If v3d_domainReply <> 1
    v3d_powerReply = v3d_MbxIdState(#V3D_TAG_SET_POWER_STATE, #V3D_POWER_ID_OLD, #V3D_MBX_STATE_ON, 1)
  EndIf

  ; --- claim the clock, then ask it to run at its ceiling ------------
  v3d_MbxIdState(#V3D_TAG_SET_CLOCK_STATE, #V3D_CLOCK_ID, #V3D_MBX_STATE_ON, 1)
  If v3d_clockMax > 0
    v3d_clockApplied = MailboxClockRateSet(#V3D_CLOCK_ID, v3d_clockMax, 0)
  EndIf

  ; --- and what it says afterwards -----------------------------------
  v3d_domainAfter     = V3dQueryDomainState()
  v3d_clockStateAfter = V3dQueryClockState()
  ; MEASURED, not configured. The firmware counts edges to produce this
  ; one, which is why the tag exists separately from GET_CLOCK_RATE, and
  ; it is the only number here that is an observation rather than an
  ; intention. A gated clock cannot be measured at a rate with jitter
  ; on it - that is the argument sdio.pi4:1760-1775 makes and it is the
  ; strongest single fact this transaction can produce.
  v3d_clockMeasured = MailboxClockRateMeasured(#V3D_CLOCK_ID)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE REGISTER SEQUENCE
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  v3d_AsbEnable - clear REQ_STOP on one bridge and wait for ACK to go
;  low.
;
;  THE POLARITY IS THE THING TO GET RIGHT AND IT READS BACKWARDS.
;  bcm2835_asb_control (bcm2835-power.c:179-186):
;
;      while (!!(readl(base + reg) & ASB_ACK) == enable) { ... }
;
;  With enable true, the loop CONTINUES while ACK is set, so it exits
;  when ACK reads ZERO. ACK here is the bridge acknowledging that it
;  HAS stopped; enabling means waiting for that acknowledgement to go
;  away. Reading it as "wait for ACK" - which is what the name invites
;  - waits forever on a bridge that came up already enabled, which on
;  a board whose firmware has touched V3D is the likely case.
;
;  Read-modify-write, not a bare store: the other bits in this register
;  belong to the bridge and the kernel preserves them (:174-177).
;
;  Returns 1 on success, 0 on timeout.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i v3d_AsbEnable(off.i)
  Protected v.i
  Protected hz.i
  Protected deadline.i
  Protected spins.i

  v = V3dAsbRead(off)
  V3dAsbWrite(off, v & (~#V3D_ASB_REQ_STOP))
  V3dBarrier()

  hz = V3dCounterHz()
  deadline = 0
  If hz > 0
    deadline = V3dTicks() + ((hz / 1000000) * #V3D_ASB_TIMEOUT_US)
  EndIf
  spins = 0
  Repeat
    v = V3dAsbRead(off)
    If (v & #V3D_ASB_ACK) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    spins = spins + 1
    If spins > #V3D_SPIN_LIMIT
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    ; Two deadlines. Written as two separate Ifs and not as one `Or`
    ; because this dialect wants a full comparison on both sides of an
    ; Or, and two Ifs read better than the correct long form here.
    If hz > 0
      If V3dTicks() > deadline
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
    EndIf
  ForEver
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  V3dBringUp() - the register-level half. Deassert reset, enable both
;  ASB bridges.
;
;  bcm2835_asb_power_on (bcm2835-power.c:287-340), reached for V3D at
;  :383-386. The two clk_prepare_enable steps are the mailbox's job and
;  V3dAskFirmware() has already done what can be done about them; what
;  is left is steps 2, 4 and 5, plus the 1 us settle between.
;
;  IT REFUSES TO RUN UNTIL THE BRIDGES HAVE ANSWERED. The PM block at
;  $FE100000 also holds PM_RSTC at $1C and PM_WDOG at $24 - the SoC's
;  reset path. If the peripheral window is not where this file thinks
;  it is, a password-bearing write at a guessed offset into that block
;  is a reboot, not a failed experiment. The bridge witness costs two
;  reads and removes that possibility, so it is a precondition and not
;  an option.
;
;  IT ALSO REFUSES IF PM_GRAFX READS ALL-ONES. That is the signature of
;  nothing answering on the bus, and it is worth its own error code
;  because it means the PM base is wrong while the ASB bases were
;  right, which is a very specific and quite likely mistake.
;
;  MASTER BEFORE SLAVE, and the order is the kernel's (:322-334): the
;  master port is the one V3D uses to reach memory, the slave port is
;  how the ARM reaches V3D's registers. Enabling the slave first would
;  make the registers readable while the block still could not fetch
;  anything, which is a state that reads as healthy and is not.
;
;  Returns #V3D_OK or the error that stopped it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i V3dBringUp()
  Protected r.i
  Protected g.i

  r = V3dBridgeCheck()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  g = V3dPmRead(#V3D_PM_GRAFX)
  v3d_grafxBefore = g
  If g = $FFFFFFFF
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_PM_DEAD
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_PM_DEAD
  EndIf

  ; Step 1 of the kernel sequence is a clock enable followed by a 1 us
  ; wait "for reset to propagate" (:304-305). The clock half went
  ; through the mailbox; the wait is real and cheap and is kept,
  ; because its purpose is to let 32 clocks of the V3D domain pass
  ; before the reset is released, and that is a property of the
  ; silicon rather than of who enabled the clock.
  V3dDelayUs(#V3D_RESET_SETTLE_US)

  ; Step 2 - deassert. Read-modify-write with the password.
  V3dPmWrite(#V3D_PM_GRAFX, g | #V3D_PM_V3DRSTN)
  V3dBarrier()
  V3dDelayUs(#V3D_RESET_SETTLE_US)

  ; READ IT BACK. A PM write without the password is silently
  ; discarded, and so is a write to an offset that is not a real
  ; register. Neither reports anything. This is the only place the
  ; mistake is visible, and catching it here turns a mystery IDENT of
  ; zero into a named refusal.
  g = V3dPmRead(#V3D_PM_GRAFX)
  v3d_grafxAfter = g
  If (g & #V3D_PM_V3DRSTN) = 0
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_RESET
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_RESET
  EndIf

  v3d_ident1AfterReset = V3dHubRead(#V3D_HUB_IDENT1)

  ; Steps 4 and 5 - the two bridges, master first.
  v3d_asbMBefore = V3dAsbRead(#V3D_ASB_V3D_M_CTRL)
  If v3d_AsbEnable(#V3D_ASB_V3D_M_CTRL) = 0
    v3d_asbMAfter = V3dAsbRead(#V3D_ASB_V3D_M_CTRL)
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_ASB_MASTER
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_ASB_MASTER
  EndIf
  v3d_asbMAfter = V3dAsbRead(#V3D_ASB_V3D_M_CTRL)

  v3d_asbSBefore = V3dAsbRead(#V3D_ASB_V3D_S_CTRL)
  If v3d_AsbEnable(#V3D_ASB_V3D_S_CTRL) = 0
    v3d_asbSAfter = V3dAsbRead(#V3D_ASB_V3D_S_CTRL)
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_ASB_SLAVE
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_ASB_SLAVE
  EndIf
  v3d_asbSAfter = V3dAsbRead(#V3D_ASB_V3D_S_CTRL)

  v3d_ident1AfterAsb = V3dHubRead(#V3D_HUB_IDENT1)
  ProcedureReturn #V3D_OK
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  STAGE 0b - IDENTITY
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  V3dReadIdent() - take the whole identity snapshot in one go.
;
;  Everything is read even when the first register already says the
;  block is dark. A wall of zeros and a wall of ones look identical in
;  a summary and completely different in a dump, and the dump costs
;  eight Device reads.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure V3dReadIdent()
  V3dBarrier()
  v3d_ident0     = V3dHubRead(#V3D_HUB_IDENT0)
  v3d_ident1     = V3dHubRead(#V3D_HUB_IDENT1)
  v3d_ident2     = V3dHubRead(#V3D_HUB_IDENT2)
  v3d_ident3     = V3dHubRead(#V3D_HUB_IDENT3)
  v3d_mmuDebug   = V3dHubRead(#V3D_MMU_DEBUG_INFO)
  v3d_coreIdent0 = V3dCoreRead(#V3D_CTL_IDENT0)
  v3d_coreIdent1 = V3dCoreRead(#V3D_CTL_IDENT1)
  v3d_coreIdent2 = V3dCoreRead(#V3D_CTL_IDENT2)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The decoders. Each takes the raw word rather than reading the
;  register again, so a diagnostic can decode a value it captured
;  earlier - which is exactly what the four snapshots are for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; TVER*10 + REV, so V3D 4.2 comes out as 42. v3d_drv.c:297-299 does the
; same arithmetic and the kernel compares against 41 and 71 elsewhere,
; so this is the number the rest of the sources are written in terms of.
Procedure.i V3dVersionOf(ident1.i)
  Protected tver.i
  Protected rev.i
  tver = (ident1 >> #V3D_IDENT1_TVER_SHIFT) & #V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE
  rev  = (ident1 >> #V3D_IDENT1_REV_SHIFT) & #V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE
  ProcedureReturn (tver * 10) + rev
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dTverOf(ident1.i)
  ProcedureReturn (ident1 >> #V3D_IDENT1_TVER_SHIFT) & #V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dRevOf(ident1.i)
  ProcedureReturn (ident1 >> #V3D_IDENT1_REV_SHIFT) & #V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dCoresOf(ident1.i)
  ProcedureReturn (ident1 >> #V3D_IDENT1_NCORES_SHIFT) & #V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dHostsOf(ident1.i)
  ProcedureReturn (ident1 >> #V3D_IDENT1_NHOSTS_SHIFT) & #V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE
EndProcedure

; The five below decode CORE 0's CTL_IDENT1 at $FEC04004, NOT the hub's
; IDENT1 at $FEC0000C. Two different registers, both called IDENT1 by
; their own source, with completely different field layouts - the
; parameter is spelled `coreIdent1` here so a call site that passed the
; wrong one reads wrong.
Procedure.i V3dSlicesOf(coreIdent1.i)
  ProcedureReturn (coreIdent1 >> #V3D_CTL_IDENT1_NSLC_SHIFT) & #V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dQpusPerSliceOf(coreIdent1.i)
  ProcedureReturn (coreIdent1 >> #V3D_CTL_IDENT1_QUPS_SHIFT) & #V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dTmusOf(coreIdent1.i)
  ProcedureReturn (coreIdent1 >> #V3D_CTL_IDENT1_NTMU_SHIFT) & #V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i V3dSemaphoresOf(coreIdent1.i)
  ProcedureReturn (coreIdent1 >> #V3D_CTL_IDENT1_NSEM_SHIFT) & #V3D_CTL_IDENT1_BYTE
EndProcedure

; "Multiples of 1kb" - v3d_regs.h:216. The register holds the count of
; kilobytes, so this returns kilobytes and the caller multiplies if it
; wants bytes. Returning bytes here would hide the unit the hardware
; actually uses.
Procedure.i V3dVpmSizeKbOf(coreIdent1.i)
  ProcedureReturn (coreIdent1 >> #V3D_CTL_IDENT1_VPM_SIZE_SHIFT) & #V3D_IDENT1_NIBBLE
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  V3dCheckIdent() - the pass criterion, applied to what was read.
;
;  Stated in advance, in the constants above, and applied here in the
;  order that makes the failure most informative:
;
;    all ones  -> nothing answers at that address. MAPPING.
;    all zeros -> the address is right, the block is dark. POWER/CLOCK.
;    wrong version -> the address is right and the part is answering
;                     and it is not what this file was written for.
;                     Print the value and stop; do not adapt.
;    wrong core count -> the same, one field further in.
;
;  The last two are refusals rather than warnings on purpose. A library
;  that shrugged at TVER=7 would go on to use V3D 4.2 register offsets
;  on a V3D 7.1 part, where TFU_CS alone moves from $400 to $700
;  (v3d_regs.h:99) - and the failure that produces is a write to
;  whatever else lives at $400, which is a much worse morning than a
;  refusal.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i V3dCheckIdent()
  If v3d_ident1 = $FFFFFFFF
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_IDENT_ONES
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_IDENT_ONES
  EndIf
  If v3d_ident1 = 0
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_IDENT_ZERO
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_IDENT_ZERO
  EndIf
  If V3dTverOf(v3d_ident1) <> #V3D_EXPECT_TVER
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_IDENT_VERSION
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_IDENT_VERSION
  EndIf
  If V3dRevOf(v3d_ident1) <> #V3D_EXPECT_REV
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_IDENT_VERSION
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_IDENT_VERSION
  EndIf
  If V3dCoresOf(v3d_ident1) <> #V3D_EXPECT_NCORES
    v3d_err = #V3D_ERR_IDENT_CORES
    ProcedureReturn #V3D_ERR_IDENT_CORES
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #V3D_OK
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  V3dInit() - THE ONE A PROGRAM CALLS
;
;  Runs the whole of stage 0 in the order that makes each failure name
;  itself, snapshotting HUB_IDENT1 at four points so that ONE bench run
;  answers the question nothing on this disk answers: does the firmware
;  leave V3D clocked and out of reset on a bare-metal boot, or does it
;  not?
;
;    v3d_ident1AtEntry    read before anything is asked or written. If
;                         this is already $124-shaped, the firmware
;                         left the block running and neither the
;                         mailbox nor the register sequence was needed.
;                         That would be the single most useful thing a
;                         first run could discover.
;    v3d_ident1AfterClock after the mailbox transactions.
;    v3d_ident1AfterReset after PM_GRAFX.V3DRSTN was deasserted.
;    v3d_ident1AfterAsb   after both bridges were enabled.
;
;  Note the deliberate asymmetry: the snapshot AT ENTRY is taken BEFORE
;  the bridge check, because reading a hub register at a bad address is
;  a read and reads are safe, and knowing what a wrong mapping returns
;  is itself information. Nothing is WRITTEN before the bridge check.
;
;  Safe to call more than once. Nothing here is a one-shot: the reset
;  deassert is idempotent, clearing REQ_STOP on an already-enabled
;  bridge is a no-op that the polarity handles, and the mailbox
;  transactions are queries plus two sets that ask for the state the
;  block is already in.
;
;  Returns #V3D_OK, or the error that stopped it. V3dError() has the
;  same value afterwards.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i V3dInit()
  Protected r.i

  v3d_ready = 0
  v3d_err   = #V3D_OK

  ; Read first, write nothing. See the note above.
  v3d_ident1AtEntry = V3dHubRead(#V3D_HUB_IDENT1)

  r = V3dBridgeCheck()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  V3dAskFirmware()
  v3d_ident1AfterClock = V3dHubRead(#V3D_HUB_IDENT1)

  r = V3dBringUp()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    ; Read the identity anyway. A bring-up that failed at the ASB while
    ; IDENT already reads correctly is a completely different finding
    ; from one where IDENT is still zero, and the difference is free.
    V3dReadIdent()
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  V3dReadIdent()
  r = V3dCheckIdent()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  v3d_ready = 1
  ProcedureReturn #V3D_OK
EndProcedure


